\section*{Individual and strong pure infiniteness}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
Proper infiniteness of all positive elements is claimed to imply strong pure infiniteness; under exactness a uniform finite-amplification version suffices, and separable nuclear algebras with that property absorb O\_infinity. The cited manuscript argues for that result; its relation to this problem is specified below.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 303, 2026-09-25): Graph shrinking and polar deformation merge hereditary positive elements while retaining norm signals in all required quotients. Exactness rules out persistent amplification obstructions, while a separate nuclear argument uses matrix norms forbidden in exact targets. Controlled comparison coefficients then eliminate arbitrary mixed entries, establishing strong diagonalization and the central-sequence absorption conclusion.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 303, 2026-09-25): The problem selects the mathematical statement summarized here, including its stated hypotheses and parameter range. The cited theorem gives a claimed proof of that assertion in full.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 303, the scope page documents the following selections. One selection derives strong pure infiniteness directly from proper infiniteness of each positive element. The fixed-amplification variant assumes exactness; no general absorption conclusion is selected without its additional hypotheses. The documented statement covers the main claim of manuscript 1. Its selected declaration also covers the clause used for this problem. Comparator files: ExactInfiniteness.lean, IndividualStrongInfiniteness.lean. Declarations: OAI.WeakPureInfiniteness.exact\_weaklyPurelyInfinite\_individuallyProperlyInfinite\_any, OAI.WeakPureInfiniteness.exact\_weaklyPurelyInfinite\_stronglyPurelyInfinite, OAI.MainGap.Manuscript.individual\_to\_strong\_main.

This site has not executed the Lean files or checked their dependencies. These descriptions report the scope of the linked declarations, rather than a new verification of the proofs.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these results to an internal model. Its release notice cautions that results lacking full formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently assessed the proofs; a solved label records the scope of the reported claim.

